IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: W.P. (Crl.) No. 68 of 2008
CITATION: (2014) 2 SCC 1
YEAR: 2014

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: P. Sathasivam, B.S. Chauhan, Ranjana P. Desai, Ranjan Gogoi, S.A. Bobde

PARTIES:
Lalita Kumari vs Govt. of U.P.

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 154 CrPC

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
mandatory fir registration, cognizable offence, preliminary inquiry, police inaction

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Held that registration of an FIR under Section 154 CrPC is mandatory if the information discloses the commission of a cognizable offence, without room for preliminary inquiry except in narrow exceptions.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 154 CrPC.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

